> Dear Professor Dr Rising,
> 
> My name is Thalita Pinheiro Soares. I am a Brazilian applied researcher, impact entrepreneur, and founder of ZeroWaste Tech, working at the intersection of sustainable business transformation, circular economy, technological innovation, and measurable social and environmental impact. I am currently preparing my application for 2027 admission to the Ph.D. in Environmental Economics at the University of Delaware’s School of Marine Science & Policy.
> 
> I am reaching out because I found an unusually strong connection between my research agenda and your work on the economic impacts of climate change, integrated assessment models, the modeling of social-environmental systems, complex systems, food and resource systems, and data-driven environmental analysis.
> 
> I was particularly interested in your articles, “The Costs of ‘Costless’ Climate Mitigation” and “Synthesis of Evidence Yields High Social Cost of Carbon Due to Structural Model Variation and Uncertainties.” Your examination of the divergence between bottom-up assessments and top-down economic models of climate-mitigation costs strongly connects with my interest in understanding the full economic, organizational, and behavioral conditions that determine whether apparently viable sustainable solutions can actually be adopted and scaled. I also found your synthesis of social-cost-of-carbon estimates—particularly its attention to structural model variation, uncertainty, incomplete damage characterization, and the integration of economic and climate evidence—especially relevant to my current work on avoided-emissions quantification, carbon accounting, and the transformation of verified environmental outcomes into credible management capabilities and market value. Although these studies address climate mitigation and environmental valuation at broader systemic scales, their empirical, systems-based, interdisciplinary, and policy-relevant foundations closely reflect the scientific direction I hope to develop through my doctoral research.
> 
> My research is grounded in a validated zero-waste business initiative developed within a Brazilian retail group, where I currently conduct technical and scientific research, coordinate access to governmental innovation funding and international awards, and contribute directly to the innovation and continuous improvement of the Group’s zero-waste process. This work seeks to transform a proven operational practice into an increasingly measurable, technologically advanced, replicable, and scalable sustainable system. The Group currently diverts more than 500 tons of waste from landfills every month, donates approximately 282.6 tons of food annually, and has already generated more than BRL 9.5 million in economic value through its social and environmental practices. More than a waste management program, the initiative has evolved into a sustainable business ecosystem built around the zero-waste model, generating employment and income for recyclers, cooperatives, and recycling companies; strengthening composting organizations and organic fertilizer producers; and maintaining partnerships with nonprofit organizations that redistribute food to vulnerable communities in Brasília, Brazil’s capital.
> 
> Building on this applied experience, the supermarket Group entrusted us with the opportunity to expand its zero-waste process internationally, granting us exclusive rights to use and further develop the method validated within its operations. With the Group’s institutional approval, I co-founded ZeroWaste Tech with its Sustainability Director, who is also a member of the company’s controlling family. The startup was conceived as a Climate Tech venture designed to transform this operational knowledge into a digital, scientifically grounded, and scalable infrastructure for the adoption of zero-waste systems across companies and markets.
> 
> The platform is being designed to provide structured learning of the zero-waste process, operational protocols, data collection and analysis, artificial intelligence for evidence-based decision-making, regulatory intelligence on national and international laws and incentives, traceability, benchmarking, and dashboards for monitoring progress, targets, and performance. It is also intended to calculate social and environmental indicators, provide step-by-step guidance for alignment with ESG standards, reporting frameworks, and certifications, and verify data that may support future carbon-credit certification processes and other environmental assets associated with greenhouse-gas reductions or avoided emissions. Our objective is to transform verified environmental outcomes into management capabilities, auditable evidence, and value-creation mechanisms for retailers and their local and international partners—expanding the zero-waste supermarket into a circular ecosystem that extends far beyond food retail and may eventually be replicated across other economic sectors.
> 
> My central research question is:
> 
> “How do leadership, behavioral change, and technology interact in the expansion and scalability of sustainable systems?”
> 
> I am currently extending this work into digital measurement, reporting, and verification; carbon accounting; avoided-emissions quantification; and the study of how verified circular and environmental outcomes may become credible management capabilities and market assets.
> 
> Given this alignment, may I ask whether you anticipate working with doctoral students entering in 2027 whose research interests lie at this intersection? I would also greatly value your perspective on whether this agenda could be a strong fit for the Ph.D. in Environmental Economics at the University of Delaware’s School of Marine Science & Policy.
> 
> Would you be available for a brief 15–20 minute video conversation? I would be grateful for the opportunity to introduce the research more clearly. I would also be pleased to send my CV, Statement of Purpose, and a concise research brief in advance, should you be open to reviewing them for context.
> 
> Thank you very much for your time and consideration.
> 
> Warm regards,
> 
